.TH TERMNAV-RELAY 1
.SH NAME
termnav-relay \- route navigation through nested SSH and tmux scopes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B termnav-relay send
.I pane direction
[\fB--client-pid\fR \fIPID\fR]
.br
.B termnav-relay send
.I window next|previous
[\fB--client-pid\fR \fIPID\fR]
.br
.B termnav-relay send
.I move left|right
[\fB--client-pid\fR \fIPID\fR]
.br
.B termnav-relay commit --tmux-socket \fIsocket\fR --client-tty \fItty\fR --client-pid \fIpid\fR
.br
.B termnav-relay ssh
[\fIssh arguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Termnav establishes one owner-only Unix socket forwarding channel on each
interactive SSH connection. Navigation requests are handled by the nearest
tmux scope and otherwise forwarded to its parent. The protocol contains only
fixed pane and window navigation operations.
.PP
Install \fBshare/termnav/sshd_config\fR as an sshd_config.d fragment on any
host that should participate, validate with \fBsshd -t\fR, and reload sshd.
.PP
Explicit SSH remote commands are eligible when their command line requests a
TTY. Termnav preserves the destination and remote command as their original
OpenSSH arguments. Commands without that explicit TTY request, configured
remote commands, and non-session control modes remain ordinary SSH.
.PP
For an eligible interactive login or commanded TTY, Termnav exports
the current relay path in the invoking client process and requests
\fBSendEnv=TERMNAV_PARENT_RELAY\fR. OpenSSH carries that value per session even
when it reuses a ControlMaster, so concurrent sessions retain distinct relay
sockets without a second connection or authentication attempt.
.PP
Termnav never changes the remote command channel or OpenSSH's normal login-shell
selection. Native Windows and other non-POSIX targets therefore continue to
receive the original command or interactive shell even when they cannot create
the remote Unix-socket forward. An effective \fBExitOnForwardFailure=yes\fR
policy is delegated unchanged without adding the optional relay. On other
unsupported servers OpenSSH may report a nonfatal remote-forward warning; the
session itself continues normally. Failure to receive usable relay metadata
makes navigation a silent no-op.
.SH TWO-PHASE COMMIT TRANSPORT
Navigation uses a two-phase commit. The originating \fBsend\fR arms a per-nonce
per-client directive file under \fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/termnav-$UID/directives/<hash>/\fR
on each hop that can handle the request. The outer hop writes the read-only
DECRQM query \fBESC [ ? 2004 $ p\fR to the originating terminal. Its ordered
reply is one of \fBESC [ ? 2004 ; 0..4 $ y\fR; tmux \fBuser-keys[9]\fR through
\fBuser-keys[13]\fR bind all five legal states to \fBtermnav-relay commit\fR.
No terminal extension or terminal-specific OSC handler is required.
When no Termnav directive is pending, the binding passes the response back to
the originating pane for compatibility with tmux versions that do not answer
application DECRQM queries themselves.
.PP
When another tmux layer lies between the terminal and the executing hop, its
commit forwards the private in-band key \fBESC [777009u\fR
(\fBuser-keys[8]\fR) into the nested client. Each commit claims the oldest
pending directive for that client (FIFO by sequence number) and either forwards
again or performs the pane/window action. Window and move scopes use stable-ID
swap logic; pane scope uses a single guarded \fBif-shell\fR (edge check and
\fBselect-pane\fR atomically) to avoid TOCTOU.
.PP
Directives carry the hop-local initiating client identity (\fBclient_tty\fR,
\fBclient_pid\fR) captured from the relay server's retained environment at
\fBssh_command\fR spawn time (authoritative \fBlist-clients\fR newest
\fBclient_activity\fR then \fBfocused\fR flag filtered to \fBTMUX_PANE\fR, or
\fB/dev/tty\fR for tmux-less hops). No origin client fields are forwarded on
the wire between hops. Commit validates the client tty is a character device
and that the tty/pid pair is still present in \fBlist-clients\fR before and
immediately before acting (fail-closed).
.PP
Each directive has a 6 s TTL (\fBDIRECTIVE_TTL\fR). On successful arm the
executing hop extends the chain's TTLs by marking committed (max of existing
expiry and now+2 s). Expiry is enforced at claim time by validating
\fBexpires\fR inside the per-socket \fB.directives.lock\fR file lock. File
creation uses \fBO_CREAT|O_EXCL\fR under the shared lock so concurrent
navigations create separate per-nonce files without last-wins drops. Normal
claimers cannot observe a write in progress because they take the same lock.
If a writer crashes mid-write, a claimer rejects and removes the invalid JSON
when it is examined, without acting (fail-closed). Claims are single-claim by
\fBflock\fR + unlink.
.PP
There is no watchdog direct-execution fallback. If the terminal does not answer
DECRQM or emit validation fails, the armed directive simply expires and
navigation is a silent no-op.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\fBtermnav-relay send\fR exits \fB0\fR when armed (handled), \fB3\fR when
declined (caller may fall back to local \fBwezterm-*\fR), \fB1\fR on transport
or other error (fail-closed, do nothing), and \fB2\fR on usage error.
\fBtermnav-relay commit\fR always exits \fB0\fR (silent, bounded runtime) —
it is invoked from a foreground tmux \fBrun-shell\fR so client input pauses
until the switch lands. Stray commits with no pending directive are no-ops.
.PP
An absent parent relay is an explicit decline so ordinary local and nested-tmux
compatibility paths continue to work. Once a parent relay is present, its
timeout or transport failure is an error and never permits fallback.
.PP
\fBsend\fR itself never mutates tmux; only \fBcommit\fR does.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERMNAV_RELAY_DIRECT
When set to \fB1\fR, a handleable \fBsend\fR that has no parent relay executes
directly without arming (used by legacy real-tmux regression tests).
.TP
.B TERMNAV_RELAY_LOG
When set, the relay server appends debug lines (\fBbegin\fR/\fBend\fR per
nonce) to the given path.
.TP
.B TERMNAV_RELAY_TEST_SKIP_TTY_VALIDATION
Test-only hook to bypass \fBS_ISCHR\fR and \fBlist-clients\fR validation for
detached tmux servers.
.SH RESIDUAL RISKS
Replies the terminal emits \fIafter\fR the commit barrier for queries from the
departing pane, and post-switch handshakes from the departing subtree, can
still misroute — the same exposure the pre-relay design had (never observed in
practice). Fixing those requires reply routing inside tmux itself. Concurrent
navigations have separate per-nonce, per-client files (no last-wins drop, no
cross-client consumption); the remaining edge is per-client FIFO
misattribution if a commit is lost — the next key from the same client
executes its oldest pending directive (bounded, expires in 6 s, mitigated by
\fBexpected_pane\fR check for \fBexecute\fR). Encoding the nonce into the
commit byte stream would eliminate this; deferred. Remote \fB/tmp/termnav-relay-<token>.sock\fR
forward path portability (Termux/no-/tmp) is a deferred known limitation —
local artifacts already use \fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR, remote \fBRemoteForward\fR
stays \fB/tmp\fR for v2. \fBps eww\fR fallback remains best-effort and
fail-closed (not a robust parser). TTL 6 s may expire on deep/slow chains —
safe no-op but \fBsend\fR may have returned armed; bound is roughly 4 hops at
6 s TTL with 2 s per-hop timeout, revisitable with a pending→committed
lifecycle.
